fix(memory): record the copy a vault failure left, and sweep the bytes a hashless door read

Round 20 review, Opus 5. Three findings, all on paths this round touched.

A decision whose vault call fails returns before it opens a transaction,
so nothing was written down: the proposal stayed pending and its manifest
row stayed ready. For a refusal that moved nothing that is right. For the
one that left the bytes under a reserved or recovery name it is how they
become unreachable -- the path the row records holds nothing, reconcile
releases the row for exactly that reason, and the last thing that could
find the file goes with it. A failure that left a copy now says so, in a
way a caller can match on rather than only read, and the three decision
entry points mark the row before returning it.

A proposal whose frontmatter will not parse is rejected through the door
bound to the bytes the pre-check hashed. Those are bytes like any other,
so the rejection now sweeps copies of them too; before, the sweep was
handed an empty hash, did nothing, and the row was retired over a copy
it could have named.

And a sweep that cannot finish no longer strands the rows it was never
asked about. A row that named no bytes contributed no hash -- it is the
crash artifact whose write never landed -- and it has to be able to
drain, the more so now that reconcile leaves a marked row alone.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
